[612] (w=227) " \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;255;140;198mI need to implement a standalone script, likely in Python. I’m thinking that the existing agent usage might not work, so I’ll create a file called compare-agent-context.py. I want to inspect model usage and how token counts   "
[613] (w=227) " \u001b[3;38;2;255;140;198maccumulate in sessions. Allocating resources in a way that's similar to how agent usage is structured seems smart. I’ll also add features like argparse for different modes and ensure the script is robust and modular. It’s     "
[614] (w=227) " \u001b[3;38;2;255;140;198mimportant to have output summaries, warnings, and debug options.\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                                                                                                  "
[615] (w=227) "                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   "
[616] (w=227) " \u001b[1m\u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;255;140;198mDesigning a Modular Script\u001b[39m\u001b[23m\u001b[22m                                                                                                                                                                                                        "
[617] (w=227) "                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   "
[618] (w=227) " \u001b[3m\u001b[38;2;255;140;198mI think it’s a good idea to include static known values from existing data, along with dynamic fetching and manual overrides. The user wants a modular script that's easy to update. I’ll implement an optional JSON file path    "
[619] (w=227) " \u001b[3;38;2;255;140;198mwith built-in defaults, even if current prices aren't known. I’ll fetch values at runtime and use minimal placeholders if necessary, warning if unavailable. The script will also allow for various active window modes and       "
[620] (w=227) " \u001b[3;38;2;255;140;198minclude commands for comparison and saving baselines. Focus is on building rather than just explaining!\u001b[39m\u001b[23m                                                                                                                           "
