{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I want to create a local testing script/dashboard to compare context efficiency between Codex and Pi, but only for the same GPT models.\n\nImplementation reference: @agent-usage\n\nImportant:\nDo not compare Codex’s total usage against Pi’s total usage blindly.\nThe experiment should compare matching GPT models only, for example:\n\n- gpt-5.3-codex in Codex vs gpt-5.3-codex in Pi\n- gpt-5.4 in Codex vs gpt-5.4 in Pi\n- gpt-5.5 in Codex vs gpt-5.5 in Pi\n\nIgnore or separate non-GPT models such as:\n\n- deepseek-v4-flash-free\n- Claude models\n- Haiku/Sonnet/Opus models\n\nThe purpose is to answer:\n\n“Given the same model family, does Pi manage context more efficiently than Codex?”\n\nContext:\nI already have a usage dashboard that reads local session history from:\n\nCodex:\n- ~/.codex/sessions\n- ~/.codex/history.jsonl\n\nPi:\n- ~/.pi/agent/sessions\n- ~/.pi may be a symlink, so resolve it safely\n\nImportant time-window issue:\nI will not use Codex for a few days while testing Pi.\nSo the script must support comparing different date windows fairly.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Codex baseline window:\n  - last 3 active Codex days\n  - or a custom range like 2026-06-04 to 2026-06-06\n\n- Pi test window:\n  - last 3 active Pi days\n  - or a custom range like 2026-06-07 to 2026-06-10\n\nDo not require both tools to be used on the exact same calendar days.\n\nInstead, support active-time-normalized comparison.\n\nCore comparison modes:\n\n1. Same calendar range:\n   Compare Codex and Pi over the same date range.\n\n2. Separate custom ranges:\n   Compare Codex over one date range and Pi over another date range.\n\n3. Last N active days per agent:\n   For example:\n   - Codex: last 3 days where Codex had meaningful activity\n   - Pi: last 3 days where Pi had meaningful activity\n\n4. Same repo filter:\n   Compare only sessions from the same repo, such as GramGrab or scripts.\n\n5. Same model filter:\n   Compare only matching GPT models.\n\nTrack and separate:\n\n- fresh input tokens\n- cached input/cache-read tokens\n- output tokens\n- total tokens = fresh input + cached + output\n- estimated API-equivalent cost\n- active time\n- session count\n- model usage\n- repo/project usage\n- reasoning effort, if available\n\nThe main metrics are:\n\n- fresh input per active hour\n- cached input per active hour\n- output per active hour\n- cost per active hour\n- fresh input per session\n- cost per session\n- cache ratio\n\nDefine cache ratio as:\n\ncached / (fresh_input + cached)\n\nThis experiment should make context waste obvious.\n\nA bad result looks like:\n\n- same GPT model\n- same/similar repo\n- similar active time\n- one agent has much higher fresh input/hour\n- one agent has much lower cache ratio\n- one agent has much higher cost/hour\n\nThe dashboard should output:\n\n1. Overall GPT-only comparison:\n   Codex GPT usage vs Pi GPT usage\n\n2. Per matching model comparison:\n   Example:\n   gpt-5.4:\n     Codex:\n       active time\n       sessions\n       fresh input\n       cached\n       output\n       total\n       cost\n       fresh input/hour\n       cache ratio\n       cost/hour\n\n     Pi:\n       same metrics\n\n     Difference:\n       Pi uses X% less/more fresh input/hour\n       Pi has X% better/worse cache ratio\n       Pi costs X% less/more per active hour\n\n3. Per repo comparison:\n   For example:\n   GramGrab:\n     Codex GPT models vs Pi GPT models\n\n4. Experiment summary:\n   Answer in plain English:\n   - Which agent used less fresh input/hour?\n   - Which agent had better cache ratio?\n   - Which agent had lower cost/hour?\n   - Was the comparison fair, or were the workloads too different?\n\n5. Fairness warnings:\n   The script should warn if:\n   - active time differs too much\n   - repo differs\n   - model differs\n   - one side used mostly gpt-5.5 while the other used mostly gpt-5.3-codex\n   - one side has too few sessions\n   - one side has too little active time\n   - cached token data is missing\n   - totals do not reconcile\n\n6. Baseline mode:\n   Since I am pausing Codex now, support saving a Codex baseline snapshot.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Save Codex baseline from 2026-06-04 to 2026-06-06\n- Later compare Pi from 2026-06-07 to 2026-06-10 against that saved baseline\n\nThe script should be robust:\n\n- Handle missing folders gracefully\n- Handle malformed JSONL lines gracefully\n- Resolve symlinks\n- Avoid crashing when a provider does not expose cached tokens\n- Make assumptions explicit\n- Keep model pricing/rates modular and easy to update\n- Do not double-count sessions\n- Do not confuse cached tokens with fresh input tokens\n- Validate that total = fresh input + cached + output\n- Warn when aggregation does not reconcile\n\nImplementation preference:\nUse TypeScript or Python, whichever is easier for parsing local files and producing a rich terminal dashboard.\n\nBuild the script, do not only explain.\nFirst inspect the local session file structures, infer schemas, then implement provider-specific parsers:\n\n- CodexParser\n- PiParser\nAdd debug mode that prints safe schema samples without exposing secrets.\n\nThe final question this script must answer is:\n\n“On the same GPT models, normalized by active time and preferably filtered to the same repo, does Pi manage context more efficiently than Codex?”"}],"timestamp":1780772475174}
